Cloud computing is the on-demand availability of computing resources, especially data storage and computing power, without direct management by the user. The components of cloud computing include:


 -  Cloud storage, which provides scalable access to data storage, typically in the form of a large, distributed file system;


 -  Cloud servers, which provide scalable, centralized computing resources;


 -  Cloud platforms, which are the software environments that run on cloud servers and allow applications to run in the cloud;


 -  Cloud applications, which are applications that run on cloud platforms and provide cloud-based services to end users.


 A diagram of these components is shown below:

The advantages of cloud computing include:


 -  Reduced cost and improved scalability of IT infrastructure;


 -  Standardized, automated deployment and operation of applications and infrastructure;


 -  Increased agility and faster time to market for new applications and services.


 The disadvantages of cloud computing include:


 -  Loss of direct control over data and applications;


 -  Potential for lower performance and availability due to shared resources;


 -  Potential for reduced security due to shared infrastructure.

Software as a Service (SaaS) is a cloud-based software delivery model in which applications are hosted in the cloud and made available to users over the internet. The benefits of SaaS include:


 -  Reduced IT infrastructure costs, as there is no need to purchase and maintain software and hardware;


 -  Improved scalability and agility, as SaaS applications can be quickly provisioned and deployed;


 -  Simplified application management, as SaaS applications are managed by the provider and users do not need to install and maintain application software;


 -  Improved security, as SaaS applications are hosted in a secure cloud environment.

The cloud deployment models identified by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) include:


 -  Public cloud, in which cloud services are provided by a service provider and made available to the general public over the internet;


 -  Private cloud, in which cloud services are provided by an organization for internal use by employees, partners, or customers;


 -  Community cloud, in which cloud services are provided by an organization for use by a community of interest, such as government agencies or academic institutions;


 -  Hybrid cloud, in which cloud services are provided by an organization using a combination of public and private cloud infrastructure.

